since taking office in January Prime  Minister Hassan Teebs government has  been working on a plan to save Lebanon's  economy that plan is now ready the state  is close to bankruptcy and Lebanon  defaulted on its debt for the first time  after much reluctance the government  intends to seek financial assistance  from the International Monetary Fund but  imagine with this plan as a stepping  stone we can build our future we will  continue with our request to the IMF and  will engage seriously with our creditors  as a result we will reduce the burden of  debt on the shoulders of our citizens  the economic hardship has been mounting  anger spilled onto the streets and it  turned violent the national currency the  pound lost more than 60 percent of its  value in recent weeks international  donors have demanded economic reforms  and then end to corruption to unlock  billions of dollars in aid while the  government's plan mentions the need for  reforms its focus seems to be on the  banking sector and taking back what the  Prime Minister calls unrealistic  interests on bank deposits over the past  five years while the government wants  the banking sector to assume the full  cost of the crisis special authorities  to restructure the banking sector this  will result in a total lack of  confidence in the Lebanese economy and  in the banking sector by an entire  generation of depositors Lebanese  politicians most of whom are accused of  involvement in corruption have traded  blame over who is responsible but the  opposition says the governing alliance  is using its power to target them in an  anti-corruption Drive  welcome italia the current government's  chosen by those in power and their  political allies unfortunately has been  turned into a tool to settle political  scores and revenge practices the power  struggle is worsening tensions the  government needs to restore the trust of  its citizens Jeb says his cabinet is  made up of independent expert  but it was brought to power by some  parties implicated in corruption  scandals that is why protesters have  been trying to topple the political  class say they have little faith and  promises to recover stolen public money  the Prime Minister may think the plan  will put the country back on track but  the concerns of many Lebanese who have  lost the value of their money have not  been eased Zenica dar al jazeera Beirut
 

